// 2016 GS1 (Prelims) Set A — Transcribed from "E:\UPSC question paper\2016\2016 PRILIMS QUES & ANSW.docx"
// Contains question, choices (A, B, C, D), answer, explanation, and upsc review points
export const paper = { year: 2016, name: 'GS1', set: 'A' };

export const questions = [
{"no":1,"text":"Which of the following statements is/are correct?\nA Bill pending in the Lok Sabha lapses on its prorogation.\nA Bill pending in the Rajya Sabha, which has not been passed by the Lok Sabha, shall not lapse on dissolution of the Lok Sabha.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Bill Lapsing","Prorogation","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Prorogation does not cause a Bill to lapse.\nDissolution of the Lok Sabha affects Bills pending in the Lok Sabha.\nA Bill pending in the Rajya Sabha does not lapse merely because the Lok Sabha is dissolved.\nTherefore, statement 1 is incorrect and statement 2 is correct.","revisionPoints":"Prorogation ≠ dissolution.\nProrogation does not lapse pending Bills.\nDissolution of Lok Sabha can cause certain Bills to lapse.\nRajya Sabha is a permanent House."},
{"no":2,"text":"Which of the following is/are the indicator/indicators used by IFPRI to compute the Global Hunger Index Report?\nUndernourishment\nChild stunting\nChild mortality","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1, 2 and 3","D":"1 and 3 only","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Global Hunger Index","IFPRI","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The Global Hunger Index uses multiple indicators of hunger and undernutrition.\nUndernourishment measures inadequate dietary energy intake.\nChild stunting reflects chronic undernutrition.\nChild mortality is also included as an indicator.","revisionPoints":"GHI includes undernourishment.\nChild indicators include stunting, wasting and mortality.\nGHI is a composite measure of hunger.\nDistinguish stunting = chronic undernutrition from wasting = acute undernutrition."},
{"no":3,"text":"There has been a persistent deficit budget year after year. Which action/actions can reduce the deficit?\nReducing revenue expenditure\nIntroducing new welfare schemes\nRationalizing subsidies\nReducing import duty","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Finance","tags":["Fiscal Deficit","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Reducing unnecessary revenue expenditure directly reduces government expenditure.\nRationalizing subsidies can reduce excessive or inefficient expenditure.\nIntroducing new welfare schemes generally increases expenditure.\nReducing import duty can reduce government revenue and therefore does not directly reduce the deficit.","revisionPoints":"Fiscal deficit = Total expenditure − Total receipts excluding borrowings.\nExpenditure reduction can reduce fiscal deficit.\nSubsidy rationalization can improve fiscal management.\nLower revenue can worsen the fiscal deficit."},
{"no":4,"text":"The establishment of \"Payment Banks\" is being allowed in India to promote financial inclusion. Which statements are correct?\nMobile companies and supermarket chains can promote Payment Banks.\nPayment Banks can issue credit cards and debit cards.\nPayment Banks cannot undertake lending activities.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"1 and 3 only","C":"2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["Payment Banks","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Payment Banks were introduced primarily to promote financial inclusion.\nEligible entities such as mobile companies and supermarket chains can promote them.\nThey can accept deposits and issue debit cards.\nThey cannot issue credit cards or undertake lending activities.","revisionPoints":"Payment Banks → deposits + payments/remittances.\nCan issue debit cards.\nCannot undertake lending.\nCannot issue credit cards under the source framework.\nImportant distinction: Payment Bank ≠ Small Finance Bank."},
{"no":5,"text":"With reference to \"LiFi\", which statements are correct?\nIt uses light for data transmission.\nIt is several times faster than WiFi.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["LiFi","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"LiFi (Light Fidelity) uses light, particularly LED light, for wireless data transmission.\nIt is a form of wireless optical communication.\nThe source describes LiFi as offering much higher speeds than conventional WiFi.","revisionPoints":"LiFi → Light-based wireless communication.\nWiFi → primarily radio-frequency communication.\nLiFi commonly uses LED light.\nUseful in environments where radio-frequency interference is undesirable."},
{"no":6,"text":"\"Intended Nationally Determined Contributions (INDCs)\" are related to","A":"Refugee rehabilitation","B":"Climate change action plans","C":"AIIB capital contribution","D":"Sustainable Development Goals","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["INDC","Paris Agreement","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"INDCs are national commitments relating to climate-change mitigation and adaptation.\nCountries submitted their intended commitments in the context of the Paris climate negotiations.\nThey became the basis for countries' climate commitments under the Paris Agreement framework.","revisionPoints":"INDC → Intended Nationally Determined Contribution.\nRelated to climate action.\nAssociated with the Paris Agreement process.\nLater commonly referred to as NDCs after countries joined the Paris Agreement."},
{"no":7,"text":"\"UDAY\" Scheme was launched for","A":"Renewable energy startups","B":"Electricity for every household","C":"Replacing coal plants","D":"Revival of power distribution companies","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["UDAY Scheme","DISCOMs","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"UDAY stands for Ujwal DISCOM Assurance Yojana.\nIt was designed to improve the financial and operational health of electricity distribution companies.\nThe scheme focused particularly on reducing the financial stress of DISCOMs.","revisionPoints":"UDAY → Ujwal DISCOM Assurance Yojana.\nDISCOM = Distribution Company.\nFocus → financial and operational turnaround.\nImportant electricity-sector reform scheme."},
{"no":8,"text":"Regarding IFC Masala Bonds:\nIFC is a World Bank institution.\nMasala Bonds are rupee-denominated bonds.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Markets","tags":["Masala Bonds","IFC","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"IFC (International Finance Corporation) is part of the World Bank Group.\nMasala Bonds are bonds denominated in Indian rupees.\nThey can be issued in international markets while being rupee-denominated.","revisionPoints":"IFC → World Bank Group institution.\nMasala Bonds → rupee-denominated bonds issued outside India.\nCurrency denomination is different from the location of issuance.\nRelated to international capital markets."},
{"no":9,"text":"Regarding taxation under Krishna Deva Raya:\nLand tax depended on land quality.\nWorkshop owners paid industry tax.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Krishna Deva Raya","Vijayanagara Taxation","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The source associates taxation under Krishna Deva Raya with assessment based on the quality of land.\nIt also mentions taxation of workshop owners as an industry-related tax.\nTherefore, both statements are correct according to the source.","revisionPoints":"Krishna Deva Raya → Vijayanagara Empire.\nLand revenue was an important source of state income.\nTaxation extended beyond agriculture.\nCrafts and occupations could also be subject to taxation."},
{"no":10,"text":"Which book contains the love story of Agnimitra, son of the founder of the Sunga dynasty?","A":"Swapnavasavadatta","B":"Malavikagnimitra","C":"Meghaduta","D":"Ratnavali","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Literature","tags":["Malavikagnimitra","Sunga Dynasty","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Malavikagnimitra is a Sanskrit play attributed to Kalidasa.\nIt revolves around the love story of King Agnimitra and Malavika.\nAgnimitra was associated with the Sunga dynasty.","revisionPoints":"Malavikagnimitra → Kalidasa.\nAgnimitra → Sunga ruler.\nIt is a Sanskrit drama.\nKalidasa's other works include Abhijnanashakuntalam and Meghaduta."},
{"no":11,"text":"\"Amber Box\", \"Blue Box\" and \"Green Box\" are related to","A":"WTO Affairs","B":"SAARC","C":"UNFCCC","D":"India-EU FTA","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Trade","tags":["WTO Subsidy Boxes","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"These terms are associated with WTO rules on agricultural subsidies.\nThey classify different types of domestic support provided by governments.\nThe classification is especially important under the Agreement on Agriculture.","revisionPoints":"Amber Box → trade-distorting domestic support.\nGreen Box → permitted support with minimal/no trade distortion.\nBlue Box → certain production-limiting programmes.\nRelated to WTO Agreement on Agriculture."},
{"no":12,"text":"Which are included in the Capital Budget?\nAsset creation\nForeign loans\nLoans to States","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Finance","tags":["Capital Budget","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Capital Budget deals with capital receipts and capital expenditure.\nCreation of assets is part of capital expenditure.\nLoans received from foreign sources are capital receipts.\nLoans and advances provided to States are capital expenditure.","revisionPoints":"Capital Budget → capital receipts + capital expenditure.\nCapital expenditure generally creates assets or reduces liabilities.\nBorrowings are capital receipts.\nLoans given by government are capital expenditure."},
{"no":13,"text":"Importance of UN Convention to Combat Desertification\nPromotes effective action.\nFocuses mainly on South Asia and North Africa.\nEncourages participation of local people.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"International Environmental Bodies","tags":["UNCCD","Desertification","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The UN Convention to Combat Desertification promotes coordinated action against desertification and land degradation.\nIt encourages participation of local communities in combating land degradation.\nIts scope is global, not restricted mainly to South Asia and North Africa.","revisionPoints":"UNCCD → United Nations Convention to Combat Desertification.\nFocus → desertification, land degradation and drought.\nLocal/community participation is important.\nNot geographically restricted to South Asia or North Africa."},
{"no":14,"text":"Which currency was proposed to be added to IMF SDR Basket?","A":"Rouble","B":"Rand","C":"Indian Rupee","D":"Renminbi","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Financial Institutions","tags":["IMF SDR Basket","Renminbi","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Renminbi (RMB) is the currency of China.\nIt was proposed for inclusion in the IMF's Special Drawing Rights (SDR) basket.\nThe SDR basket is composed of major international currencies.","revisionPoints":"SDR → international reserve asset created by the IMF.\nRenminbi = Chinese currency.\nSDR basket represents major international currencies.\nSDR is not itself a conventional currency."},
{"no":15,"text":"Regarding IMFC:\nAdvises IMF.\nWorld Bank attends as observer.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Financial Institutions","tags":["IMFC","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"IMFC stands for International Monetary and Financial Committee.\nIt provides advice and guidance to the IMF on the international monetary and financial system.\nThe World Bank participates as an observer.","revisionPoints":"IMFC → advisory body associated with the IMF.\nDiscusses international monetary and financial issues.\nIt does not function as the IMF's executive board.\nWorld Bank participates as an observer."},
{"no":16,"text":"Rashtriya Garima Abhiyan aims to","A":"Rehabilitate homeless","B":"Rehabilitate sex workers","C":"Eradicate manual scavenging","D":"Rehabilitate bonded labourers","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["Rashtriya Garima Abhiyan","Manual Scavenging","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Rashtriya Garima Abhiyan focuses on ending manual scavenging.\nIt is associated with dignity, rehabilitation and elimination of degrading sanitation-related labour practices.\nThe initiative seeks social rehabilitation along with eradication of the practice.","revisionPoints":"Manual scavenging → social justice issue.\nFocus → eradication + rehabilitation.\nLinked with dignity and livelihood rehabilitation.\nDo not confuse with schemes specifically targeting bonded labour."},
{"no":17,"text":"Which statements are correct?\nSiddhas were monotheistic and opposed idol worship.\nLingayats rejected caste hierarchy and rebirth.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Siddhas","Lingayats","Bhakti Movement","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The source considers the statement regarding Siddhas as incorrect.\nLingayats challenged caste hierarchy and rejected the conventional idea of rebirth.\nTherefore, only statement 2 is correct according to the source.","revisionPoints":"Lingayat movement → associated with Basavanna.\nCritiqued caste hierarchy.\nEmphasized devotion to Shiva.\nMedieval Bhakti movements often challenged rigid social hierarchies."},
{"no":18,"text":"\"Import Cover\" means","A":"Import/GDP ratio","B":"Total imports","C":"Export-import ratio","D":"Number of months imports can be financed using forex reserves","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Import Cover","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Import cover measures the adequacy of a country's foreign exchange reserves.\nIt indicates approximately how many months of imports can be financed using available forex reserves.\nHigher import cover generally indicates greater external liquidity protection.","revisionPoints":"Import cover → Forex reserves ÷ imports.\nExpressed generally in months.\nUsed as an indicator of external-sector resilience.\nDifferent from export-import ratio."},
{"no":19,"text":"Which pairs are correctly matched?\nKurd — Bangladesh\nMadhesi — Nepal\nRohingya — Myanmar","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 only","C":"2 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Kurds","Madhesi","Rohingya","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Madhesis are a major ethnic/social group associated with Nepal's Terai region.\nRohingya are an ethnic Muslim minority historically associated with Myanmar, particularly Rakhine State.\nThe source does not match Kurds with Bangladesh.","revisionPoints":"Madhesi → Nepal's Terai region.\nRohingya → Myanmar/Rakhine context.\nKurds → associated mainly with West Asia, particularly Turkey, Iraq, Iran and Syria.\nEthnic identity and nationality are not always synonymous."},
{"no":20,"text":"Regarding OPCW:\nEU organization\nMonitors chemical industry\nProvides assistance against chemical weapons","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Organizations","tags":["OPCW","Chemical Weapons","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"OPCW stands for Organisation for the Prohibition of Chemical Weapons.\nIt is an international organization, not an EU organization.\nIt monitors relevant chemical-industry activities under the Chemical Weapons Convention.\nIt also provides assistance and support against chemical-weapon threats.","revisionPoints":"OPCW → Organisation for the Prohibition of Chemical Weapons.\nAssociated with the Chemical Weapons Convention (CWC).\nWorks toward eliminating chemical weapons.\nImportant functions include verification, monitoring and assistance."},
{"no":21,"text":"Regarding PM Fasal Bima Yojana\nUniform 2% premium for every crop.\nCovers post-harvest losses.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["PM Fasal Bima Yojana","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"PM Fasal Bima Yojana provides crop insurance coverage to farmers.\nThe premium rates are not uniformly 2% for every crop.\nThe scheme includes coverage for specified post-harvest losses.\nTherefore, only statement 2 is correct.","revisionPoints":"PMFBY → crop insurance scheme.\nPremium is not identical for all crops.\nProvides protection against specified crop losses.\nPost-harvest coverage is an important feature."},
{"no":22,"text":"Great Indian Hornbill is naturally found in","A":"Thar Desert","B":"Himalayas","C":"Rann of Kutch","D":"Western Ghats","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Great Indian Hornbill","Western Ghats","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The Great Indian Hornbill is associated with forest ecosystems.\nIt is naturally found in regions including the Western Ghats.\nThe Western Ghats are an important biodiversity hotspot.","revisionPoints":"Great Indian Hornbill → large forest bird.\nWestern Ghats → important habitat.\nHornbills are important seed dispersers.\nHabitat loss is a major conservation concern."},
{"no":23,"text":"National Ganga River Basin Authority (NGRBA)\nRiver basin is the planning unit.\nLeads river conservation.\nChief Ministers become Chairman by rotation.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Water Resources","tags":["NGRBA","Ganga","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The river basin approach is used as a planning framework.\nNGRBA was established to coordinate conservation efforts for the Ganga.\nThe statement regarding Chief Ministers becoming Chairman by rotation is incorrect according to the source.","revisionPoints":"River basin → important unit for integrated water management.\nNGRBA → associated with Ganga conservation.\nBasin-level planning considers interconnected water resources.\nDo not confuse institutional leadership with rotational State-level leadership."},
{"no":24,"text":"Why is Neem-coated Urea promoted?","A":"Increases nitrogen fixation","B":"Slows dissolution of urea","C":"Prevents nitrous oxide emission completely","D":"Combination of fertilizer and weedicide","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Neem-coated Urea","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Neem coating slows the release/dissolution of urea.\nThis can improve the efficiency with which nitrogen is available to crops.\nIt also helps reduce the misuse or diversion of urea.\nIt does not completely prevent nitrous oxide emissions.","revisionPoints":"Neem-coated urea → controlled nitrogen release.\nImproves nitrogen-use efficiency.\nHelps reduce excessive application.\nDo not interpret it as a complete solution to nitrogen-related emissions."},
{"no":25,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThe Chief Secretary in a State is appointed by the Governor.\nThe Chief Secretary has a fixed tenure.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Public Administration","tags":["Chief Secretary","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The Chief Secretary is appointed by the State Government, not directly by the Governor.\nThere is no constitutionally fixed tenure for the Chief Secretary.\nTherefore, both statements are incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Chief Secretary → senior-most key administrative official of a State.\nAppointment is by the State Government.\nTenure is not constitutionally fixed.\nChief Secretary plays a central role in State administration."},
{"no":26,"text":"With reference to the \"Stand Up India Scheme\", which of the following statements is/are correct?\nIts purpose is to promote entrepreneurship among SC/ST and women entrepreneurs.\nIt provides for refinance through SIDBI.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["Stand Up India Scheme","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Stand Up India promotes entrepreneurship among SC/ST and women entrepreneurs.\nIt facilitates bank loans for setting up enterprises.\nSIDBI provides refinance-related support under the scheme.","revisionPoints":"Stand Up India → entrepreneurship promotion.\nTarget groups → SC/ST + women entrepreneurs.\nSIDBI → important institutional support.\nDistinguish from Start-up India, which has a broader startup ecosystem focus."},
{"no":27,"text":"The FAO accords the status of \"Globally Important Agricultural Heritage Systems (GIAHS)\" to traditional agricultural systems. What is the overall goal?\nProvide modern technology and financial support.\nIdentify and safeguard traditional farming systems, biodiversity and indigenous knowledge.\nProvide GI status to agricultural produce.","A":"1 and 3 only","B":"2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["GIAHS","FAO","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"GIAHS focuses on identifying and conserving traditional agricultural systems.\nIt recognizes their associated biodiversity, landscapes and indigenous knowledge.\nGIAHS is not the same as Geographical Indication (GI) status for products.\nThe source identifies statement 2 as the correct statement.","revisionPoints":"GIAHS → FAO initiative.\nFocus → traditional agricultural systems.\nConserves agrobiodiversity and indigenous knowledge.\nGIAHS ≠ GI tag."},
{"no":28,"text":"Which of the following is/are tributary/tributaries of the Brahmaputra?\nDibang\nKameng\nLohit","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Brahmaputra","Tributaries","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Dibang, Kameng and Lohit are important tributaries of the Brahmaputra.\nThese rivers drain parts of the northeastern Himalayan region.\nThey contribute significantly to the Brahmaputra river system.","revisionPoints":"Dibang → Arunachal Pradesh.\nLohit → major eastern tributary.\nKameng → joins the Brahmaputra system in Assam.\nBrahmaputra basin covers a major part of Northeast India."},
{"no":29,"text":"The term \"Core Banking Solutions\" best describes\nNetworking of bank branches enabling customers to access accounts from any branch.\nIncreasing RBI's control over banks through computerization.\nProcedure for takeover of banks with NPAs.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["Core Banking Solutions","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Core Banking Solutions connect bank branches through a centralized banking system.\nCustomers can access their accounts from different branches rather than being restricted to one branch.\nHence, it enables \"Anywhere Banking.\"\nStatements 2 and 3 do not describe CBS.","revisionPoints":"CBS → centralized banking network.\nEnables anywhere banking.\nUses centralized databases and connected branches.\nNot related to takeover of banks with NPAs."},
{"no":30,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\nAnnex-I Countries — Cartagena Protocol\nCertified Emission Reductions — Nagoya Protocol\nClean Development Mechanism — Kyoto Protocol","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["Kyoto Protocol","Clean Development Mechanism","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The Clean Development Mechanism (CDM) is established under the Kyoto Protocol.\nThe Cartagena Protocol deals mainly with biosafety and living modified organisms.\nThe Nagoya Protocol concerns access to genetic resources and benefit sharing.\nTherefore, only pair 3 is correctly matched.","revisionPoints":"Kyoto Protocol → climate change.\nCDM → Kyoto Protocol mechanism.\nCartagena Protocol → biosafety.\nNagoya Protocol → access and benefit sharing.\nAnnex-I → UNFCCC classification of developed/transition economies."},
{"no":31,"text":"In Bioinformatics, the term \"Transcriptome\" refers to","A":"Enzymes used in genome editing","B":"Complete set of mRNA molecules expressed by an organism","C":"Mechanism of gene expression","D":"Mechanism of genetic mutation","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["Transcriptome","Bioinformatics","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The transcriptome represents the collection of RNA transcripts expressed by a cell or organism under particular conditions.\nThe source specifically defines it in terms of the complete set of mRNA molecules.\nIt reflects which genes are actively expressed.","revisionPoints":"Genome → total genetic material.\nTranscriptome → expressed RNA transcripts.\nProteome → complete set of proteins.\nTranscriptome can vary with cell type, developmental stage and environmental conditions."},
{"no":32,"text":"\"Mission Indradhanush\" pertains to","A":"Immunization of children and pregnant women","B":"Smart Cities","C":"Search for Earth-like planets","D":"New Education Policy","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["Mission Indradhanush","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Mission Indradhanush is associated with immunization.\nIt focuses on increasing vaccination coverage among children and pregnant women.\nIt targets populations that have been missed or partially covered by routine immunization.","revisionPoints":"Mission Indradhanush → immunization.\nTarget → children and pregnant women.\nObjective → improve full immunization coverage.\nPart of India's public-health intervention framework."},
{"no":33,"text":"Which best describes the aim of Green India Mission?\nGreen accounting\nSecond Green Revolution\nRestoring and enhancing forest cover through adaptation and mitigation","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["Green India Mission","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Green India Mission focuses on forest cover and ecosystem restoration.\nIt also addresses climate change through adaptation and mitigation measures.\nIt is not a programme for green accounting or a second agricultural revolution.","revisionPoints":"Green India Mission → one of India's National Action Plan on Climate Change missions.\nFocus → forest cover and ecosystem services.\nIncludes adaptation + mitigation.\nForest restoration has climate and biodiversity benefits."},
{"no":34,"text":"Under Food Safety and Standards Regulations, mandatory information on food labels includes\nList of ingredients\nNutrition information\nMedical recommendations on allergies\nVegetarian/Non-vegetarian symbol","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"2, 3 and 4","C":"1, 2 and 4","D":"1 and 4 only","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Corporate Regulation","tags":["FSSAI","Food Labelling","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Food labels provide important information about ingredients.\nNutritional information is also required.\nVegetarian/non-vegetarian identification is part of food labelling requirements.\nThe source does not include medical allergy recommendations as a mandatory category.","revisionPoints":"Food labelling → consumer information and safety.\nIngredients → composition of food.\nNutrition information → nutritional value.\nVegetarian/non-vegetarian symbol → important labelling requirement."},
{"no":35,"text":"\"Project Loon\" is related to","A":"Waste management","B":"Wireless communication technology","C":"Solar power","D":"Water conservation","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["Project Loon","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Project Loon used high-altitude balloons to provide internet connectivity.\nThe concept aimed to extend internet access to areas with limited terrestrial infrastructure.\nIt is therefore associated with wireless communication technology.","revisionPoints":"Project Loon → high-altitude balloons.\nObjective → provide internet connectivity.\nUses stratospheric platforms.\nRelated to telecommunications and digital connectivity."},
{"no":36,"text":"\"Net Metering\" is associated with","A":"Solar energy generation by households","B":"Piped natural gas","C":"CNG kits","D":"Water meters","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Renewable Energy","tags":["Net Metering","Solar Energy","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Net metering is commonly associated with rooftop solar systems.\nElectricity generated by a consumer can be used by the consumer, while excess electricity can be supplied to the grid according to applicable regulations.\nThe system measures the net electricity exchanged with the grid.","revisionPoints":"Net metering → grid-connected electricity generation.\nCommonly used with rooftop solar.\nConsumers can offset part of their electricity consumption.\nEncourages distributed renewable-energy generation."},
{"no":37,"text":"Ease of Doing Business Index was published by","A":"OECD","B":"World Economic Forum","C":"World Bank","D":"WTO","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Financial Institutions","tags":["Ease of Doing Business","World Bank","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The World Bank published the Ease of Doing Business rankings/index.\nIt assessed the regulatory environment affecting businesses across economies.\nThe source identifies the World Bank as the publisher.","revisionPoints":"Ease of Doing Business → World Bank.\nAssessed business regulatory environments.\nCovered areas such as starting a business, permits and access to credit.\nThe World Bank later discontinued the Doing Business report."},
{"no":38,"text":"Banjaras during medieval India were generally","A":"Agriculturists","B":"Warriors","C":"Weavers","D":"Traders","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Banjaras","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Banjaras were traditionally associated with trade and transport.\nThey travelled in groups and transported goods over long distances.\nThey played an important role in supplying goods, including food grains, to different regions.","revisionPoints":"Banjaras → mobile trading/transport communities.\nImportant in medieval and early-modern trade networks.\nTransported goods across long distances.\nOften travelled in large caravans."},
{"no":39,"text":"Who first deciphered the edicts of Emperor Ashoka?","A":"Georg Bühler","B":"James Prinsep","C":"Max Müller","D":"William Jones","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["James Prinsep","Ashoka Edicts","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"James Prinsep successfully deciphered the Brahmi script used in many Ashokan inscriptions.\nThis breakthrough helped historians identify Ashoka from inscriptions that referred to him as Devanampriya Piyadasi.\nIt was a major development in the study of ancient Indian history.","revisionPoints":"James Prinsep → decipherment of Brahmi.\nAshokan inscriptions → important primary sources.\nAshoka used titles such as Devanampriya and Piyadasi.\nInscriptions provide evidence about Mauryan administration and Dhamma."},
{"no":40,"text":"With reference to the Gram Nyayalaya Act, which statements are correct?\nGram Nyayalayas hear only civil cases.\nLocal social activists may serve as mediators/reconciliators.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["Gram Nyayalaya","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Gram Nyayalayas are designed to provide accessible and inexpensive justice at the grassroots level.\nThey can hear both civil and criminal cases within their jurisdiction.\nLocal social activists can assist in mediation/reconciliation.\nTherefore, statement 1 is incorrect and statement 2 is correct.","revisionPoints":"Gram Nyayalayas → grassroots-level justice.\nCan deal with civil and criminal matters.\nEmphasis on accessibility and inexpensive justice.\nMediation/reconciliation is an important feature.\nUPSC – Questions 41–60"},
{"no":41,"text":"With reference to the Trans-Pacific Partnership (TPP)\nIt is an agreement among all Pacific Rim countries except China and Russia.\nIt is only a maritime security alliance.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Regional Organizations","tags":["Trans-Pacific Partnership","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"TPP was a trade and economic agreement, not a maritime security alliance.\nIt did not include all Pacific Rim countries.\nTherefore, both statements are incorrect.","revisionPoints":"TPP → trade/economic partnership.\nNot a military or maritime security alliance.\nIt involved selected countries of the Asia-Pacific region.\nTrade agreements and security alliances should not be confused."},
{"no":42,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThe 2015 India-Africa Summit was the third summit.\nIt was initiated by Jawaharlal Nehru in 1951.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["India-Africa Forum Summit","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The 2015 India-Africa Forum Summit was the third such summit.\nThe statement attributing its initiation to Jawaharlal Nehru in 1951 is incorrect according to the source.\nTherefore, only statement 1 is correct.","revisionPoints":"2015 → Third India-Africa Forum Summit.\nIndia-Africa relations involve political, economic and developmental cooperation.\nDistinguish historical India-Africa solidarity from the later formal summit framework.\nIndia has longstanding engagement with African countries."},
{"no":43,"text":"MCLR (Marginal Cost of Funds based Lending Rate) aims to\nImprove transparency in interest rate determination.\nEnsure fair lending rates for banks and borrowers.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["MCLR","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"MCLR was introduced to improve the transmission of policy rates to lending rates.\nIt was intended to make the determination of lending rates more transparent.\nIt also aimed to make lending rates more responsive to changes in banks' funding costs.","revisionPoints":"MCLR → Marginal Cost of Funds based Lending Rate.\nIntroduced by RBI.\nLinked lending rates more closely to marginal funding costs.\nImportant for monetary policy transmission."},
{"no":44,"text":"What is unique about the Kharai Camel?\nIt can swim in seawater.\nIt grazes on mangroves.\nIt cannot be domesticated.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Kharai Camel","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The Kharai camel is adapted to coastal environments.\nIt can swim through seawater to reach islands and mangrove areas.\nIt feeds on mangrove vegetation.\nThe statement that it cannot be domesticated is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Kharai camel → associated with coastal Gujarat.\nKnown for swimming in seawater.\nFeeds on mangroves.\nAdaptation to coastal ecosystems makes it distinctive."},
{"no":45,"text":"A newly discovered banana species with orange-coloured pulp was found in","A":"Andaman Islands","B":"Anaimalai Forests","C":"Maikala Hills","D":"Tropical rain forests of Northeast India","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Banana Species","Andaman Islands","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The source identifies the Andaman Islands as the location where the banana species with orange-coloured pulp was discovered.\nThe question tests the association between a biological discovery and its geographic location.","revisionPoints":"Andaman Islands → part of India's island biodiversity.\nTropical island ecosystems can contain unique plant species.\nGeographic location is important in biodiversity-based UPSC questions.\nRemember island biodiversity separately from mainland biodiversity hotspots."},
{"no":46,"text":"INS Astradharini is a","A":"Amphibious warfare ship","B":"Nuclear-powered submarine","C":"Torpedo launch and recovery vessel","D":"Nuclear-powered aircraft carrier","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Defence Technology","tags":["INS Astradharini","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"INS Astradharini is a specialised naval vessel.\nIts primary role is associated with the launch and recovery of torpedoes.\nIt is therefore not a submarine or aircraft carrier.","revisionPoints":"INS Astradharini → torpedo launch and recovery vessel.\nAssociated with the Indian Navy.\nSpecialised support vessels perform testing and recovery functions.\nDo not confuse support vessels with frontline combat platforms."},
{"no":47,"text":"\"Greased Lightning-10 (GL-10)\" is","A":"Electric plane tested by NASA","B":"Solar-powered aircraft by Japan","C":"Chinese space observatory","D":"Reusable rocket by ISRO","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Transport Technology","tags":["Electric Aircraft","NASA","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"GL-10 refers to an experimental electric aircraft.\nIt was developed and tested by NASA.\nIt demonstrated the potential of electric propulsion in aircraft technology.","revisionPoints":"GL-10 → electric aircraft.\nAssociated with NASA.\nImportant in the field of electric aviation.\nElectric propulsion can reduce dependence on conventional aviation fuels."},
{"no":48,"text":"Regarding \"Initiative for Nutritional Security through Intensive Millets Promotion\"\nDemonstrates improved production and post-harvest technologies.\nPoor, small, marginal and tribal farmers are the major beneficiaries.\nProvides free nutrient kits and micro-irrigation equipment to encourage millet cultivation.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["INSIMP","Millets","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The initiative focuses on improving millet production and post-harvest technologies.\nPoor, small, marginal and tribal farmers are important beneficiaries.\nThe source does not support the claim that it provides free nutrient kits and micro-irrigation equipment as stated in statement 3.","revisionPoints":"Millet promotion → linked with nutrition and food security.\nMillets are generally hardy and suitable for relatively dry conditions.\nSmall and marginal farmers are important beneficiaries.\nPost-harvest technology is important for reducing losses and improving value."},
{"no":49,"text":"\"Swadeshi\" and \"Boycott\" were first adopted during the","A":"Anti-Partition of Bengal Movement","B":"Home Rule Movement","C":"Non-Cooperation Movement","D":"Simon Commission Protest","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Swadeshi Movement","Partition of Bengal","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The Swadeshi and Boycott movements became prominent during the anti-partition movement following the partition of Bengal in 1905.\nSwadeshi encouraged the use of indigenous goods.\nBoycott encouraged rejection of foreign goods.","revisionPoints":"Partition of Bengal → 1905.\nSwadeshi → promotion of indigenous goods.\nBoycott → rejection of foreign goods.\nThese became important methods of nationalist mobilisation."},
{"no":50,"text":"With reference to Buddhism, consider the following statements:\nThe concept of Bodhisattva is central to Hinayana Buddhism.\nBodhisattva is a compassionate being on the path to enlightenment.\nBodhisattva delays personal Nirvana to help all sentient beings.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Bodhisattva","Mahayana Buddhism","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The Bodhisattva ideal is associated particularly with Mahayana Buddhism, not Hinayana/Theravada.\nA Bodhisattva is understood as a being committed to attaining enlightenment while helping other sentient beings.\nThe ideal emphasizes compassion and universal liberation.","revisionPoints":"Bodhisattva ideal → Mahayana Buddhism.\nKey concept → compassion.\nTheravada traditionally emphasizes the Arhat ideal.\nMahayana places greater emphasis on universal liberation."},
{"no":51,"text":"\"Doctors Without Borders (Médecins Sans Frontières)\", often in the news, is","A":"A division of the World Health Organization","B":"A non-governmental international organization","C":"An inter-governmental agency sponsored by the European Union","D":"A specialized agency of the United Nations","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Organizations","tags":["Doctors Without Borders","MSF","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Médecins Sans Frontières (MSF) is an independent humanitarian organization.\nIt provides medical assistance in areas affected by conflict, disasters and humanitarian crises.\nIt is not a UN specialized agency or a WHO division.","revisionPoints":"MSF → international humanitarian NGO.\nProvides medical assistance.\nOften operates in conflict and disaster zones.\nIndependent from governments and international governmental organizations."},
{"no":52,"text":"With reference to \"The Economics of Ecosystems and Biodiversity (TEEB)\", which statements are correct?\nIt is hosted by UNEP, IMF and World Economic Forum.\nIt focuses on the economic benefits of biodiversity.\nIt helps decision-makers recognize and value ecosystems.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"International Environmental Bodies","tags":["TEEB","UNEP","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"TEEB focuses on the economic value of biodiversity and ecosystem services.\nIt seeks to help policymakers recognize the economic consequences of biodiversity loss.\nThe source identifies TEEB as a UNEP initiative, not an initiative of IMF and WEF.","revisionPoints":"TEEB → The Economics of Ecosystems and Biodiversity.\nFocus → economic valuation of biodiversity.\nEcosystem services can have significant economic value.\nHelps integrate biodiversity considerations into decision-making."},
{"no":53,"text":"With reference to \"Red Sanders\", consider the following statements:\nIt is found in a part of South India.\nIt is one of the most important trees in tropical rain forests of South India.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Red Sanders","Eastern Ghats","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Red Sanders is found in South India, particularly associated with the Eastern Ghats.\nIt is not described as a major tree of the tropical rain forests of South India.\nThe source specifically identifies it as endemic to the Eastern Ghats.","revisionPoints":"Red Sanders → Pterocarpus santalinus.\nAssociated with the Eastern Ghats.\nEndemic to a restricted geographical region.\nImportant high-value timber species."},
{"no":54,"text":"Proper implementation of the UN-REDD+ Programme contributes to\nProtection of biodiversity\nResilience of forest ecosystems\nPoverty reduction","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["UN-REDD+","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"REDD+ is associated with reducing emissions from deforestation and forest degradation.\nProper implementation can support forest conservation and biodiversity.\nHealthy forests can strengthen ecosystem resilience.\nForest-dependent communities can also benefit through appropriate livelihood mechanisms.","revisionPoints":"REDD+ → Reducing Emissions from Deforestation and Forest Degradation.\nFocus → forests + climate mitigation.\nCan generate biodiversity benefits.\nLocal and forest-dependent communities are important stakeholders."},
{"no":55,"text":"What is the \"Greenhouse Gas Protocol\"?","A":"International accounting tool for measuring and managing greenhouse gas emissions","B":"UN financial incentive programme","C":"International treaty to reduce emissions","D":"REDD+ initiative hosted by the World Bank","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["Greenhouse Gas Protocol","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The Greenhouse Gas Protocol provides frameworks for measuring and reporting greenhouse gas emissions.\nIt helps organizations account for emissions systematically.\nIt is not itself an international treaty.","revisionPoints":"GHG Protocol → emissions accounting framework.\nHelps measure and report greenhouse gas emissions.\nUsed by organizations and businesses.\nDo not confuse it with UN climate treaties."},
{"no":56,"text":"With reference to the Financial Stability and Development Council (FSDC), consider the following:\nIt is an organ of NITI Aayog.\nIt is headed by the Union Finance Minister.\nIt monitors macro-prudential supervision of the economy.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["FSDC","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"FSDC is not an organ of NITI Aayog.\nIt is headed by the Union Finance Minister.\nIt deals with financial stability and macro-prudential supervision.\nTherefore, statements 2 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"FSDC → Financial Stability and Development Council.\nHeaded by Union Finance Minister.\nFocus → financial stability and macro-prudential supervision.\nNITI Aayog and FSDC are separate institutions."},
{"no":57,"text":"With reference to \"Agenda 21\", consider the following statements:\nIt is a global action plan for sustainable development.\nIt originated at the Johannesburg Summit (2002).","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Sustainable Development","tags":["Agenda 21","Rio Earth Summit","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Agenda 21 is a comprehensive global action plan for sustainable development.\nIt was adopted at the Rio Earth Summit in 1992.\nTherefore, statement 1 is correct and statement 2 is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Agenda 21 → Rio Earth Summit, 1992.\nJohannesburg Summit → 2002.\nAgenda 21 focuses on sustainable development.\nRemember the sequence: Rio 1992 → Johannesburg 2002."},
{"no":58,"text":"Satya Shodhak Samaj organized","A":"Tribal movement in Bihar","B":"Temple-entry movement in Gujarat","C":"Anti-caste movement in Maharashtra","D":"Peasant movement in Punjab","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Satya Shodhak Samaj","Jyotiba Phule","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Satyashodhak Samaj was founded by Jyotirao Phule.\nIt challenged caste-based social inequality and Brahmanical dominance.\nIts activities were particularly significant in Maharashtra.","revisionPoints":"Satyashodhak Samaj → founded by Jyotirao Phule.\nFounded in 1873.\nFocus → social equality and anti-caste reform.\nImportant movement in the history of social reform in Maharashtra."},
{"no":59,"text":"Viruses can infect\nBacteria\nFungi\nPlants","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Viruses","Bacteriophages","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Viruses can infect bacteria, where they are called bacteriophages.\nSome viruses infect fungi, known as mycoviruses.\nNumerous viruses infect plants.\nTherefore, all three statements are correct.","revisionPoints":"Bacteriophage → virus infecting bacteria.\nMycovirus → virus infecting fungi.\nPlant viruses → infect plant cells.\nViruses require a host cell for replication."},
{"no":60,"text":"\"Base Erosion and Profit Shifting (BEPS)\" is related to","A":"Mining operations","B":"Curbing tax avoidance by multinational companies","C":"Genetic resource exploitation","D":"Environmental cost accounting","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Taxation","tags":["BEPS","Tax Avoidance","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"BEPS refers to strategies used by multinational enterprises to shift profits to jurisdictions with lower tax rates or erode taxable income.\nInternational efforts aim to reduce such practices and ensure that profits are appropriately taxed.\nTherefore, BEPS is associated with international taxation.","revisionPoints":"BEPS → Base Erosion and Profit Shifting.\nMajor issue in international taxation.\nAssociated with multinational enterprises.\nOECD/G20 cooperation is important in BEPS reforms."},
{"no":61,"text":"India's first National Investment and Manufacturing Zone (NIMZ) was proposed in","A":"Andhra Pradesh","B":"Gujarat","C":"Maharashtra","D":"Uttar Pradesh","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["NIMZ","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"India's first NIMZ was proposed in Andhra Pradesh.\nNIMZs are intended to promote large-scale manufacturing.\nThey aim to create integrated infrastructure and improve the manufacturing ecosystem.","revisionPoints":"NIMZ → National Investment and Manufacturing Zone.\nFocus → manufacturing and industrial development.\nDesigned to provide integrated infrastructure.\nImportant in India's manufacturing-policy framework."},
{"no":62,"text":"District Mineral Foundations (DMFs) are meant for\nPromoting mineral exploration.\nProtecting interests of mining-affected people.\nAuthorizing mineral exploration licences.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Mining & Minerals","tags":["District Mineral Foundations","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"DMFs are established in mining-affected districts.\nTheir primary purpose is to work for the interest and benefit of people affected by mining.\nThey do not function as authorities for granting mineral exploration licences.","revisionPoints":"DMF → District Mineral Foundation.\nFocus → mining-affected communities.\nIntended to address social and developmental impacts of mining.\nDo not confuse DMF with the authority responsible for granting mining licences."},
{"no":63,"text":"\"SWAYAM\" aims at","A":"Promoting Self Help Groups","B":"Funding start-ups","C":"Education and health of adolescent girls","D":"Providing free quality education through online learning","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["SWAYAM","Digital India","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"SWAYAM is an online learning initiative.\nIt seeks to provide access to quality educational courses through digital platforms.\nIt is associated with expanding access to education through online learning.","revisionPoints":"SWAYAM → online education initiative.\nUses digital platforms for learning.\nPromotes wider access to educational content.\nImportant concept under digital education."},
{"no":64,"text":"The Montagu-Chelmsford Proposals were related to","A":"Social reforms","B":"Educational reforms","C":"Police administration reforms","D":"Constitutional reforms","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Montagu-Chelmsford Reforms","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The Montagu-Chelmsford Proposals dealt with constitutional and administrative reforms in British India.\nThey formed the basis of the Government of India Act, 1919.\nThe Act introduced dyarchy in the provinces.","revisionPoints":"Montagu-Chelmsford Reforms → 1919.\nAssociated with Government of India Act, 1919.\nIntroduced dyarchy in provinces.\nImportant milestone in constitutional development."},
{"no":65,"text":"What is common to Ajanta and Mahabalipuram?\nBuilt during the same period.\nBelong to the same religion.\nBoth have rock-cut monuments.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"None of the above","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"UNESCO Heritage","tags":["Ajanta","Mahabalipuram","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Both Ajanta and Mahabalipuram contain rock-cut monuments.\nThey were not constructed during exactly the same period.\nTheir religious associations are also not identical.\nTherefore, only statement 3 is correct.","revisionPoints":"Ajanta → famous for Buddhist caves and paintings.\nMahabalipuram → associated with Pallava architecture.\nBoth contain rock-cut monuments.\nRock-cut architecture involves carving directly into natural rock."},
{"no":66,"text":"With reference to Bitcoins, which statements are correct?\nBitcoins are tracked by central banks.\nAnyone with a Bitcoin address can send and receive Bitcoins.\nOnline payments can be made without either party knowing the other's identity.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["Bitcoin","Cryptocurrency","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Bitcoin transactions operate on a decentralized blockchain network, rather than being controlled by a central bank.\nA Bitcoin address can be used to send and receive bitcoins.\nTransactions use addresses rather than necessarily revealing the real-world identity of the parties.\nTherefore, statements 2 and 3 are correct according to the source.","revisionPoints":"Bitcoin → decentralized digital currency.\nTransactions recorded on a blockchain.\nBitcoin addresses are not equivalent to real-world identities.\nBlockchain provides a public transaction record."},
{"no":67,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nNew Development Bank was established by APEC.\nThe headquarters of New Development Bank is in Shanghai.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Financial Institutions","tags":["New Development Bank","BRICS","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The New Development Bank (NDB) was established by the BRICS countries, not APEC.\nIts headquarters are located in Shanghai, China.\nTherefore, only statement 2 is correct.","revisionPoints":"NDB → established by BRICS.\nHeadquarters → Shanghai.\nFocus → infrastructure and sustainable development.\nAPEC and BRICS are different international groupings."},
{"no":68,"text":"\"Gadgil Committee Report\" and \"Kasturirangan Committee Report\" are related to","A":"Constitutional reforms","B":"Ganga Action Plan","C":"Linking of rivers","D":"Protection of Western Ghats","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Environmental Law","tags":["Western Ghats","Gadgil Committee","Kasturirangan Committee","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Both committees examined issues related to the ecological protection of the Western Ghats.\nThe reports differed in their approaches to balancing conservation and development.\nThey are therefore important in India's environmental-policy debates.","revisionPoints":"Gadgil Committee → Western Ghats ecology.\nKasturirangan Committee → Western Ghats conservation.\nKey issue → environment vs development.\nWestern Ghats → major biodiversity-rich region."},
{"no":69,"text":"Keshab Chandra Sen is associated with the establishment of\nCalcutta Unitarian Committee\nTabernacle of New Dispensation\nIndian Reform Association","A":"1 and 3 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Keshab Chandra Sen","Brahmo Samaj","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Keshab Chandra Sen was an important leader associated with the Brahmo movement.\nHe established the Tabernacle of New Dispensation.\nHe was also associated with the Indian Reform Association.\nThe source does not associate him with the Calcutta Unitarian Committee.","revisionPoints":"Keshab Chandra Sen → Brahmo Samaj movement.\nAssociated with religious and social reform.\nNew Dispensation → Navavidhan.\nIndian Reform Association → social reform activities."},
{"no":70,"text":"Which of the following is not a member of the Gulf Cooperation Council (GCC)?","A":"Iran","B":"Saudi Arabia","C":"Oman","D":"Kuwait","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Regional Organizations","tags":["Gulf Cooperation Council","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The GCC consists of six Gulf Arab monarchies.\nSaudi Arabia, Oman and Kuwait are members.\nIran is not a GCC member.","revisionPoints":"GCC members:\nBahrain\nKuwait\nOman\nQatar\nSaudi Arabia\nUAE\nIran → not a GCC member.\nGCC → regional organization of Gulf Arab states."},
{"no":71,"text":"The objectives of the Sovereign Gold Bond Scheme and Gold Monetization Scheme are\nMobilize idle household gold.\nPromote FDI in gold and jewellery.\nReduce dependence on gold imports.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Markets","tags":["Sovereign Gold Bond","Gold Monetization Scheme","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Both schemes seek to make India's large stock of privately held gold more productive.\nThey aim to mobilize idle gold.\nThey also seek to reduce India's dependence on imported physical gold.\nPromoting FDI in gold and jewellery is not their stated core objective.","revisionPoints":"Sovereign Gold Bond → investment linked to gold prices without holding physical gold.\nGold Monetization → mobilizes physical gold.\nBoth can reduce demand for imported physical gold.\nRelated to improving the productive use of gold holdings."},
{"no":72,"text":"\"Belt and Road Initiative\" is associated with","A":"African Union","B":"Brazil","C":"European Union","D":"China","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Belt and Road Initiative","China","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The Belt and Road Initiative (BRI) is an international infrastructure and connectivity initiative associated with China.\nIt includes projects involving transport, trade and other forms of connectivity across participating countries.","revisionPoints":"BRI → associated with China.\nFocus → connectivity and infrastructure.\nIncludes land and maritime connectivity concepts.\nImportant in discussions on international trade and geopolitics."},
{"no":73,"text":"Pradhan Mantri MUDRA Yojana aims at","A":"Bringing small entrepreneurs into the formal financial system","B":"Providing crop loans to farmers","C":"Providing pensions to elderly people","D":"Funding voluntary organizations","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["Pradhan Mantri MUDRA Yojana","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"PM MUDRA Yojana focuses on providing credit to micro and small businesses.\nIt aims to bring small entrepreneurs into the formal financial system.\nIt supports income-generating activities through institutional credit.","revisionPoints":"MUDRA → Micro Units Development and Refinance Agency.\nFocus → micro enterprises.\nProvides institutional credit support.\nImportant for financial inclusion and entrepreneurship."},
{"no":74,"text":"In which regions of India are shale gas resources found?\nCambay Basin\nCauvery Basin\nKrishna-Godavari Basin","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Mining & Minerals","tags":["Shale Gas","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Shale gas resources have been identified in multiple sedimentary basins of India.\nThe Cambay, Cauvery and Krishna-Godavari Basins are among the regions associated with shale-gas potential.\nTherefore, all three statements are correct according to the source.","revisionPoints":"Shale gas → unconventional natural gas.\nFound within shale formations.\nRequires specialized extraction techniques.\nCambay, Cauvery and Krishna-Godavari are important sedimentary basins."},
{"no":75,"text":"The \"Global Financial Stability Report\" is prepared by","A":"European Central Bank","B":"International Monetary Fund (IMF)","C":"World Bank","D":"OECD","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Financial Institutions","tags":["Global Financial Stability Report","IMF","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The IMF publishes the Global Financial Stability Report.\nIt assesses risks and vulnerabilities in the global financial system.\nThe report is published periodically to evaluate international financial stability.","revisionPoints":"GFSR → IMF.\nFocus → global financial stability.\nExamines financial risks and vulnerabilities.\nDo not confuse it with the World Bank's development-focused reports."},
{"no":76,"text":"Regarding \"Atal Pension Yojana\", which of the following statements is/are correct?\nIt is a minimum guaranteed pension scheme mainly targeted at unorganized sector workers.\nOnly one member of a family can join the scheme.\nThe same amount of pension is guaranteed for the spouse for life after the subscriber's death.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["Atal Pension Yojana","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"APY provides a guaranteed minimum pension.\nIt is particularly aimed at workers in the unorganized sector.\nMore than one eligible family member can independently subscribe.\nAfter the subscriber's death, the spouse is entitled to the same pension according to the scheme provisions.","revisionPoints":"APY → Atal Pension Yojana.\nFocus → pension coverage, especially for unorganized-sector workers.\nProvides a guaranteed pension amount based on contribution.\nEligible family members can join separately."},
{"no":77,"text":"The term \"Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership (RCEP)\" appears in the news in the context of","A":"G20","B":"ASEAN","C":"SCO","D":"SAARC","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Regional Organizations","tags":["RCEP","ASEAN","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"RCEP is a major free trade agreement associated with ASEAN and its dialogue partners.\nASEAN played a central role in initiating and negotiating the arrangement.\nIt connects ASEAN with major economies in the Asia-Pacific region.","revisionPoints":"RCEP → Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership.\nASEAN-centered trade arrangement.\nIncludes major Asia-Pacific economies.\nFocus → trade and economic integration."},
{"no":78,"text":"On which of the following can you find the Bureau of Energy Efficiency (BEE) Star Label?\nCeiling fans\nElectric geysers\nTubular fluorescent lamps","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Renewable Energy","tags":["BEE Star Label","Energy Efficiency","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The BEE Star Label indicates the energy efficiency of covered appliances and equipment.\nCeiling fans, electric geysers and tubular fluorescent lamps are among the products to which such efficiency labelling can apply.\nThe label helps consumers compare energy performance.","revisionPoints":"BEE → Bureau of Energy Efficiency.\nStar rating → energy-efficiency indicator.\nHigher star rating generally indicates greater efficiency.\nHelps consumers make energy-conscious purchasing decisions."},
{"no":79,"text":"India is an important member of the International Thermonuclear Experimental Reactor (ITER). If the experiment succeeds, what is the immediate advantage for India?","A":"Thorium can replace uranium for power generation.","B":"India can play a global role in satellite navigation.","C":"India can improve the efficiency of fission reactors.","D":"India can build fusion reactors for power generation.","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Nuclear Technology","tags":["ITER","Fusion Reactors","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"ITER is an international project focused on demonstrating the feasibility of nuclear fusion.\nFusion is fundamentally different from conventional nuclear fission.\nSuccessful fusion technology could contribute to future fusion-based power generation.","revisionPoints":"ITER → International Thermonuclear Experimental Reactor.\nFocus → nuclear fusion.\nFusion is the process powering the Sun.\nFission → splitting heavy nuclei; fusion → combining light nuclei."},
{"no":80,"text":"With reference to the history of India, consider the following pairs:\nEripatti – Land whose revenue was set apart for the maintenance of village tanks.\nTaniyurs – Villages donated to a single Brahmin or a group of Brahmins.\nGhatikas – Colleges generally attached to temples.","A":"1 and 2","B":"3 only","C":"2 and 3","D":"1 and 3","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Chola Administration","Eripatti","Taniyurs","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Eripatti refers to land whose revenue was assigned for the maintenance of village tanks.\nGhatikas were educational institutions, generally associated with temples.\nThe description given for Taniyurs is incorrect; hence only statements 1 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Eripatti → revenue linked to tank maintenance.\nGhatikas → centres of learning/education.\nTaniyur → a distinctive administrative/village category in South Indian history.\nThese terms are useful for understanding South Indian temple and village institutions.\nUPSC – Questions 81–100"},
{"no":81,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThe International Solar Alliance was launched at the UN Climate Change Conference in 2015.\nThe Alliance includes all the member countries of the United Nations.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Renewable Energy","tags":["International Solar Alliance","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The International Solar Alliance (ISA) was launched in 2015 during the UN Climate Change Conference.\nThe initiative was initially open to countries located between the Tropics of Cancer and Capricorn.\nTherefore, the statement that it included all UN members is incorrect according to the source.","revisionPoints":"ISA → International Solar Alliance.\nLaunched in 2015.\nFocus → promotion of solar energy.\nIndia played a key role in launching the initiative.\nInitial membership concept was linked to the tropical region."},
{"no":82,"text":"\"European Stability Mechanism\" is","A":"An agency to manage the refugee crisis.","B":"An agency providing financial assistance to Eurozone countries.","C":"An agency dealing with trade agreements.","D":"An agency resolving disputes among EU members.","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Regional Organizations","tags":["European Stability Mechanism","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The European Stability Mechanism (ESM) is a financial assistance mechanism for Eurozone countries.\nIt was created to provide financial support to members facing serious financial difficulties.\nIt is therefore associated with financial stability, not refugee management or trade disputes.","revisionPoints":"ESM → European Stability Mechanism.\nFocus → financial stability of the Eurozone.\nProvides financial assistance to eligible Eurozone members.\nImportant during European sovereign-debt crises."},
{"no":83,"text":"Which of the following are advantages of drip irrigation?\nReduction in weed growth.\nReduction in soil salinity.\nReduction in soil erosion.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"None of the above","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Drip Irrigation","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Drip irrigation delivers water directly near the root zone.\nThis reduces water application to areas where weeds may grow, helping reduce weed growth.\nControlled water application can also reduce soil erosion.\nAccording to the source, reduction in soil salinity is not taken as a general advantage in this question.","revisionPoints":"Drip irrigation → micro-irrigation technique.\nWater is delivered near plant roots.\nReduces water wastage.\nCan reduce weed growth and soil erosion.\nParticularly useful where water is scarce."},
{"no":84,"text":"Regarding \"DigiLocker\", which statements are correct?\nIt is a digital locker system under the Digital India Programme.\nIt allows access to e-documents from anywhere.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["DigiLocker","Digital India","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"DigiLocker is a digital document-storage and access platform under the Digital India Programme.\nIt enables users to access digital documents electronically.\nThis reduces dependence on physical copies of documents.","revisionPoints":"DigiLocker → digital document platform.\nPart of Digital India.\nEnables electronic access to documents.\nUseful for paperless governance and digital public services."},
{"no":85,"text":"Recently, linking of which rivers was undertaken?","A":"Cauvery and Tungabhadra","B":"Godavari and Krishna","C":"Mahanadi and Sone","D":"Narmada and Tapti","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Water Resources","tags":["River Interlinking","Godavari","Krishna","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The source identifies the Godavari–Krishna river-linking project.\nSuch interlinking projects aim to transfer water between river basins.\nThey are associated with water-resource management and regional water availability.","revisionPoints":"Godavari → major Peninsular river.\nKrishna → major Peninsular river.\nRiver interlinking → transfer of water between basins.\nKey issues include irrigation, water availability, ecology and displacement."},
{"no":86,"text":"Which atmospheric gases are considered while calculating the Air Quality Index (AQI)?\nCarbon dioxide\nCarbon monoxide\nNitrogen dioxide\nSulfur dioxide\nMethane","A":"1, 2 and 3 only","B":"2, 3 and 4 only","C":"1, 4 and 5 only","D":"1, 2, 3, 4 and 5","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["Air Quality Index","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The source identifies carbon monoxide (CO), nitrogen dioxide (NO₂) and sulfur dioxide (SO₂) among the pollutants considered for AQI.\nCarbon dioxide and methane are important greenhouse gases but are not included in the set given for this AQI question.\nTherefore, statements 2, 3 and 4 are correct.","revisionPoints":"AQI → indicator of ambient air quality.\nCO → toxic combustion-related pollutant.\nNO₂ → major pollutant associated with combustion.\nSO₂ → commonly associated with sulfur-containing fuels.\nGreenhouse gas ≠ automatically an AQI pollutant."},
{"no":87,"text":"With reference to \"Astrosat\", consider the following statements:\nOther than USA and Russia, India is the only country to have launched such an observatory.\nAstrosat is a 2000 kg satellite placed at an orbit of 1650 km.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["Astrosat","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The source marks both statements as incorrect.\nThe numerical description of Astrosat in statement 2 is incorrect.\nThe claim in statement 1 about India being the only country besides the USA and Russia is also incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Astrosat → India's multi-wavelength space observatory.\nStudies celestial objects in different electromagnetic bands.\nSpace observatories allow observations beyond atmospheric limitations.\nAvoid memorizing unsupported satellite specifications without checking the source."},
{"no":88,"text":"In medieval India, \"Araghatta\" refers to","A":"Bonded labour","B":"Military land grants","C":"Waterwheel used for irrigation","D":"Cultivated wasteland","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Araghatta","Irrigation","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Araghatta refers to a water-lifting device or waterwheel used for irrigation.\nSuch technologies helped raise water from wells or other sources for agricultural use.\nIt reflects the development of irrigation technology in medieval India.","revisionPoints":"Araghatta → waterwheel/water-lifting device.\nAssociated with irrigation.\nImportant for agricultural expansion.\nShows the role of technology in medieval agriculture."},
{"no":89,"text":"The memorizing of chronicles, dynastic histories and epic tales was the profession of","A":"Shramana","B":"Parivraajaka","C":"Agrahaarika","D":"Maagadha","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Maagadha","Bards","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Maagadhas were associated with preserving and reciting chronicles, dynastic histories and epic traditions.\nTheir role involved oral transmission of historical and cultural traditions.\nTherefore, Maagadha is the correct answer.","revisionPoints":"Maagadha → associated with oral historical traditions.\nPreserved dynastic and epic narratives.\nImportant in understanding ancient Indian traditions of historical memory.\nOral traditions were important sources before widespread written records."},
{"no":90,"text":"Which State first declared a butterfly as its \"State Butterfly\"?","A":"Arunachal Pradesh","B":"Himachal Pradesh","C":"Karnataka","D":"Maharashtra","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["State Butterfly","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"According to the source, Himachal Pradesh was the first State to declare a butterfly as its State Butterfly.\nState symbols are often used to promote awareness and conservation of local biodiversity.","revisionPoints":"State Butterfly → biodiversity and conservation symbol.\nHimachal Pradesh → identified by the source as the first State to declare one.\nState symbols can increase public awareness.\nButterfly diversity is closely linked with habitat quality."},
{"no":91,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding Mangalyaan:\nIt is also called Mars Orbiter Mission.\nIt made India the second country to orbit Mars after the USA.\nIndia became the first country to successfully reach Mars orbit on its maiden attempt.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["Mangalyaan","Mars Orbiter Mission","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Mangalyaan is also known as the Mars Orbiter Mission (MOM).\nIndia became the first country to successfully reach Mars orbit on its first attempt.\nTherefore, statement 3 is correct.\nStatement 2 is incorrect because India was not the second country to orbit Mars after the USA.","revisionPoints":"Mangalyaan = Mars Orbiter Mission.\nLaunched by ISRO.\nEntered Mars orbit in 2014.\nIndia achieved Mars orbit on its maiden attempt.\nImportant milestone in India's planetary exploration."},
{"no":92,"text":"The main reason for the split in the Indian National Congress at Surat (1907) was","A":"Introduction of communalism by Lord Minto.","B":"Extremists lost faith in the Moderates' methods.","C":"Formation of Muslim League.","D":"Aurobindo Ghosh could not become President.","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Surat Split 1907","Indian National Congress","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The Surat split reflected growing differences between the Moderates and Extremists within the Congress.\nExtremists increasingly disagreed with the moderate approach to constitutional agitation.\nThe disagreement over leadership and methods contributed to the division.","revisionPoints":"Surat Split → 1907.\nMain division → Moderates vs Extremists.\nExtremists favoured stronger methods of political mobilisation.\nImportant leaders associated with the Extremist tendency included Bal Gangadhar Tilak, Lala Lajpat Rai and Bipin Chandra Pal."},
{"no":93,"text":"Sir Stafford Cripps' Plan proposed that after World War II","A":"India should receive complete independence.","B":"India should first be partitioned.","C":"India should become a republic in the Commonwealth.","D":"India should receive Dominion Status.","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Cripps Plan","Dominion Status","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The Cripps Mission came to India in 1942 during World War II.\nIts proposals offered Dominion Status after the war.\nThe proposals also provided for the possibility of provinces choosing not to join the proposed Indian Union.","revisionPoints":"Cripps Mission → 1942.\nHeaded by Sir Stafford Cripps.\nProposed Dominion Status after World War II.\nCongress rejected the proposals.\nIt preceded the Quit India Movement of 1942."},
{"no":94,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\nFamous Place\nRegion\n1. Bodhgaya\nBaghelkhand\n2. Khajuraho\nBundelkhand\n3. Shirdi\nVidarbha\n4. Nashik\nMalwa\n5. Tirupati\nRayalaseema\nWhich are correctly matched?","A":"1, 2 and 4","B":"2, 3, 4 and 5","C":"2 and 5 only","D":"1, 3, 4 and 5","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Famous Places","Regions of India","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Khajuraho is associated with the Bundelkhand region.\nTirupati is associated with the Rayalaseema region.\nThe other pairings given are incorrect according to the source.\nHence, pairs 2 and 5 are correctly matched.","revisionPoints":"Khajuraho → Bundelkhand.\nTirupati → Rayalaseema.\nRegional geography is frequently tested through place-region matching.\nLearn historical-cultural regions separately from present-day State boundaries."},
{"no":95,"text":"Parliament can legislate on a State List subject in the national interest if a resolution is passed by","A":"Lok Sabha by simple majority.","B":"Lok Sabha by two-thirds majority.","C":"Rajya Sabha by simple majority.","D":"Rajya Sabha by a majority of not less than two-thirds of members present and voting.","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Centre-State Relations","tags":["Article 249","State List","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"Under Article 249, Parliament can legislate on a State List matter if the Rajya Sabha passes a resolution declaring it necessary in the national interest.\nThe resolution requires a majority of not less than two-thirds of members present and voting.\nThe power is therefore linked specifically to the Rajya Sabha.","revisionPoints":"Article 249 → Parliament legislates on State List in national interest.\nResolution passed by Rajya Sabha.\nRequired majority → 2/3 of members present and voting.\nResolution remains effective for the period prescribed by the Constitution and can be renewed."},
{"no":96,"text":"Which State explored constructing an artificial inland port connected to the sea by a navigational channel?","A":"Andhra Pradesh","B":"Chhattisgarh","C":"Karnataka","D":"Rajasthan","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Infrastructure & Transport","tags":["Inland Port","Rajasthan","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The source identifies Rajasthan as the State that explored the idea of an artificial inland port connected to the sea through a navigational channel.\nThe question highlights the possibility of creating inland connectivity despite Rajasthan being a landlocked State.","revisionPoints":"Rajasthan → landlocked State.\nInland waterways can provide transport connectivity.\nPort development is usually associated with coastal access.\nArtificial inland-port concepts can involve canals or navigational channels."},
{"no":97,"text":"With reference to the Paris Agreement (2015), consider the following:\nIt was signed by all UN member countries and came into force in 2017.\nIt aims to keep global temperature rise well below 2°C and pursue efforts to limit it to 1.5°C.\nDeveloped countries committed to mobilize US$100 billion per year to support developing countries.","A":"1 and 3 only","B":"2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["Paris Agreement","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The Paris Agreement aims to keep the increase in global average temperature well below 2°C and pursue efforts to limit it to 1.5°C.\nThe climate-finance commitment of US$100 billion per year for developing countries is associated with developed-country commitments.\nStatement 1 is incorrect because the Agreement was not simply signed by all UN members as stated, and it entered into force in 2016, not 2017.","revisionPoints":"Paris Agreement → 2015.\nAdopted under the UNFCCC.\nTemperature goals → well below 2°C + pursue 1.5°C.\nEntered into force in 2016.\nClimate finance is a major element of developed–developing country negotiations."},
{"no":98,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nSustainable Development Goals (SDGs) were first proposed in 1972 by the Club of Rome.\nSDGs are to be achieved by 2030.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Sustainable Development","tags":["SDGs","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The SDGs are part of the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development.\nThey are intended to be achieved by 2030.\nThey were not first proposed in 1972 by the Club of Rome.","revisionPoints":"SDGs → 17 Sustainable Development Goals.\nAdopted by UN Member States in 2015.\nTarget year → 2030.\nSDGs cover social, economic and environmental dimensions of development."},
{"no":99,"text":"The Man Who Knew Infinity is based on the life of","A":"Srinivasa Ramanujan","B":"Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar","C":"Satyendra Nath Bose","D":"C. V. Raman","ans":"A","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Books & Authors","tags":["Srinivasa Ramanujan","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The Man Who Knew Infinity is based on the life of Indian mathematician Srinivasa Ramanujan.\nIt portrays his mathematical work and association with mathematician G. H. Hardy at Cambridge.\nRamanujan made major contributions to number theory and related areas of mathematics.","revisionPoints":"Srinivasa Ramanujan → renowned Indian mathematician.\nAssociated with number theory.\nWorked with G. H. Hardy.\nBorn in Erode and raised in Tamil Nadu.\nHis contributions remain influential in modern mathematics."},
{"no":100,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThe minimum age to become a member of a Panchayat is 25 years.\nA Panchayat reconstituted after premature dissolution continues only for the remainder of the original term.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Local Self-Government","tags":["Panchayat","UPSC","2016"],"expl":"The minimum age to contest Panchayat elections is 21 years, not 25.\nWhen a Panchayat is dissolved prematurely, the reconstituted Panchayat normally serves only for the remaining period of the original term.\nTherefore, statement 1 is incorrect and statement 2 is correct.","revisionPoints":"Panchayats → constitutional status through the 73rd Constitutional Amendment.\nMinimum age to contest → 21 years.\nNormal Panchayat term → 5 years.\nReconstituted Panchayat after premature dissolution generally serves the remaining term.\nPanchayati Raj → Part IX of the Constitution."},
];
